INSERT INTO `node_revision__body` VALUES
('page',0,2,3,'en',0,'<h2>About Qatra</h2><p><strong>Qatra</strong> (“drop” in Persian) is a nonprofit organization that restores dignity, livelihood, and hope to Afghan women and children through grassroots initiatives. We stand with those who are silenced, displaced, and oppressed, especially under Taliban rule.</p><h3>Our Mission</h3><p>To support and restore economic and personal agency to Afghan women and children through education, skills training, employment, and sustainable livelihoods.</p><h3>Our Vision</h3><p>A future where Afghan women and children thrive in dignity, independence, and safety.</p><h3>Core Values</h3><ul><li>Dignity</li><li>Equity</li><li>Courage</li><li>Community</li><li>Creativity</li><li>Transparency</li></ul><h3>The Story Behind Qatra</h3><p>Qatra was born from the idea that even the smallest act of compassion, a single drop, can form an ocean of change. Founded by a woman who grew up witnessing the silent resilience of Afghan women, Qatra carries the legacy of love, justice, and resistance.</p>','','basic_html'),
('page',0,2,4,'en',0,'<h2>About Qatra</h2><p><strong>Qatra</strong> (“drop” in Persian) is a nonprofit organization that restores dignity, livelihood, and hope to Afghan women and children through grassroots initiatives. We stand with those who are silenced, displaced, and oppressed, especially under Taliban rule.</p><h3>Our Mission</h3><p>To support and restore economic and personal agency to Afghan women and children through education, skills training, employment, and sustainable livelihoods.</p><h3>Our Vision</h3><p>A future where Afghan women and children thrive in dignity, independence, and safety.</p><h3>Core Values</h3><ul><li>Dignity</li><li>Equity</li><li>Courage</li><li>Community</li><li>Creativity</li><li>Transparency</li></ul><h3>The Story Behind Qatra</h3><p>Qatra was born from the idea that even the smallest act of compassion, a single drop, can form an ocean of change. Founded by a woman who grew up witnessing the silent resilience of Afghan women, Qatra carries the legacy of love, justice, and resistance.</p>','','basic_html'),
('page',0,6,8,'en',0,'<h3>Meet the Heart of Qatra</h3><p>At Qatra, our greatest strength lies in our people.</p><p>From the skilled artisans who pour love and tradition into every stitch, to the women finding renewed hope and independence through their craft, our community is woven together by resilience, creativity, and shared purpose.</p><p>Each woman who joins Qatra brings with her a story — of heritage, courage, and dreams for a brighter future. Together, they uplift not only their own families, but entire communities, creating ripples of change that reach far beyond our workshops.</p><p>We stand proud of the diverse talents, warm spirits, and unwavering determination of our team. Every handcrafted piece carries the soul of the incredible women behind it, turning small drops — <em>qatra</em> — into a flowing river of hope and possibility.</p><p>Thank you for being part of their journey.</p>','','basic_html'),
('page',0,6,26,'en',0,'<h3>Meet the Heart of Qatra</h3><p>At Qatra, our greatest strength lies in our people.</p><p>From the skilled artisans who pour love and tradition into every stitch, to the women finding renewed hope and independence through their craft, our community is woven together by resilience, creativity, and shared purpose.</p><p>Each woman who joins Qatra brings with her a story — of heritage, courage, and dreams for a brighter future. Together, they uplift not only their own families, but entire communities, creating ripples of change that reach far beyond our workshops.</p><p>We stand proud of the diverse talents, warm spirits, and unwavering determination of our team. Every handcrafted piece carries the soul of the incredible women behind it, turning small drops — <em>qatra</em> — into a flowing river of hope and possibility.</p><p>Thank you for being part of their journey.</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,10,15,'en',0,'<h3><strong>Hosai Ayubi</strong></h3><p><strong>Founder of Qatra | Author | Law Graduate | Master’s Student in Theology, Political Science &amp; Philosophy | Advocate for Afghan Women</strong></p><p>Hosai Aruba is the visionary behind Qatra, passionately dedicated to uplifting Afghan women through dignity-driven work and cultural preservation. With a background in law and an academic journey spanning theology, political science, and philosophy, Hosai Aruba brings a deeply rooted understanding of justice, human rights, and the power of knowledge to her mission.</p><p>As an author and vocal advocate for Afghan women’s rights, she stands at the forefront of campaigns like #LetAfghanGirlsLearn, striving to restore education, opportunity, and hope to countless girls. Her diverse experiences—from mentoring university students, coordinating entrepreneurial programs, to volunteering for cultural heritage restoration—reflect a lifelong commitment to empowering individuals and fostering vibrant communities.</p><p>Through Qatra, Hosai Ayubi transforms small drops of effort into waves of change, championing artistry, independence, and brighter futures for women across Afghanistan.<br>&nbsp;</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,10,16,'en',0,'<h3><strong>Hosai Ayubi</strong></h3><p><strong>Founder of Qatra | Author | Law Graduate | Master’s Student in Theology, Political Science &amp; Philosophy | Advocate for Afghan Women</strong></p><p>Hosai Aruba is the visionary behind Qatra, passionately dedicated to uplifting Afghan women through dignity-driven work and cultural preservation. With a background in law and an academic journey spanning theology, political science, and philosophy, Hosai Aruba brings a deeply rooted understanding of justice, human rights, and the power of knowledge to her mission.</p><p>As an author and vocal advocate for Afghan women’s rights, she stands at the forefront of campaigns like #LetAfghanGirlsLearn, striving to restore education, opportunity, and hope to countless girls. Her diverse experiences—from mentoring university students, coordinating entrepreneurial programs, to volunteering for cultural heritage restoration—reflect a lifelong commitment to empowering individuals and fostering vibrant communities.</p><p>Through Qatra, Hosai Ayubi transforms small drops of effort into waves of change, championing artistry, independence, and brighter futures for women across Afghanistan.<br>&nbsp;</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,10,17,'en',0,'<h3><strong>Hosai Ayubi</strong></h3><p><strong>Founder of Qatra | Author | Law Graduate | Master’s Student in Theology, Political Science &amp; Philosophy | Advocate for Afghan Women</strong></p><p>Hosai Aruba is the visionary behind Qatra, passionately dedicated to uplifting Afghan women through dignity-driven work and cultural preservation. With a background in law and an academic journey spanning theology, political science, and philosophy, Hosai Aruba brings a deeply rooted understanding of justice, human rights, and the power of knowledge to her mission.</p><p>As an author and vocal advocate for Afghan women’s rights, she stands at the forefront of campaigns like #LetAfghanGirlsLearn, striving to restore education, opportunity, and hope to countless girls. Her diverse experiences—from mentoring university students, coordinating entrepreneurial programs, to volunteering for cultural heritage restoration—reflect a lifelong commitment to empowering individuals and fostering vibrant communities.</p><p>Through Qatra, Hosai Ayubi transforms small drops of effort into waves of change, championing artistry, independence, and brighter futures for women across Afghanistan.<br>&nbsp;</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,11,18,'en',0,'<ol><li><strong>Sheren: A Mother, A Warrior, A Dreamer</strong></li></ol><p>In the heart of Kabul, within the walls of a humble rented home, lives a woman whose resilience humbles all who meet her. She lives with seven family members, including her mentally disabled and paralyzed son, whom she cares for with deep love and unyielding patience.</p><p>Despite her age and the heavy weight of responsibility, she stands as the sole breadwinner of her family. Through handcrafting, something she once learned out of necessity, she now sews not only fabric but fragments of hope, dignity, and survival.</p><p>Her dream of education and becoming a teacher was stolen by poverty early in life, yet the wisdom in her hands and the stories behind her stitches speak volumes. She is a woman who never chose to give up, even when the world gave her every reason to.</p><p>Joining the Marhama Project is, for her, a kindling of hope, a flame lit in the darkness of hardship. It offers her a chance to survive these difficult times and to provide for her family with pride, even in her old age.</p><p><strong>Her Message:&nbsp;</strong></p><p>Her message to Afghan women is simple but powerful: \"Be strong. Find reasons to be happy, even when life is hard.\"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And to the world, she says: \"Do not give up on Afghan women. Hold our hands as we try to stand again.\"</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,11,19,'en',0,'<p><strong>Sheren: A Mother, A Warrior, A Dreamer</strong></p><p>In the heart of Kabul, within the walls of a humble rented home, lives a woman whose resilience humbles all who meet her. She lives with seven family members, including her mentally disabled and paralyzed son, whom she cares for with deep love and unyielding patience.</p><p>Despite her age and the heavy weight of responsibility, she stands as the sole breadwinner of her family. Through handcrafting, something she once learned out of necessity, she now sews not only fabric but fragments of hope, dignity, and survival.</p><p>Her dream of education and becoming a teacher was stolen by poverty early in life, yet the wisdom in her hands and the stories behind her stitches speak volumes. She is a woman who never chose to give up, even when the world gave her every reason to.</p><p>Joining the Marhama Project is, for her, a kindling of hope, a flame lit in the darkness of hardship. It offers her a chance to survive these difficult times and to provide for her family with pride, even in her old age.</p><p><strong>Her Message:&nbsp;</strong></p><p>Her message to Afghan women is simple but powerful: \"Be strong. Find reasons to be happy, even when life is hard.\"</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>And to the world, she says: \"Do not give up on Afghan women. Hold our hands as we try to stand again.\"</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,12,20,'en',0,'<p><strong>Jamila:&nbsp; A Mother, An Artist, A Fighter</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Jamila is one of the courageous women at the heart of Marhama.</p><p>She is mother of two young children, doing everything she can to keep them safe, warm, and fed, despite facing unimaginable odds.</p><p>Born into a life of poverty in Bamiyan, Jamila grew up in a home shaped by hardship. Her father was disabled, and her mother was often ill.</p><p>From a young age, the weight of responsibility rested on her small shoulders. At just 11 years old, in 5th grade, she learned embroidery from her neighbors. It wasn\'t just a craft for her, it was survival. She balanced school, working on farm, and embroidery work to help support her parents and siblings.</p><p>Her resilience led her all the way to Kabul University, a dream many girls in her situation are never allowed to dream. Even while attending university, she continued to stitch late into the night just to afford her education. She carried hope in every thread, hope for a future she could build for herself and her family. But after the collapse of the system, her dreams were abruptly halted. She is no longer allowed to work. With no steady income and two children to feed, Jamila tried to find occasional embroidery work in her neighborhood, though it barely pays.</p><p>Joining Marhama project means more than just income for Jamila. It has brought back a sense of purpose, of dignity.</p><p>It is a light in the darkness, a chance to feed her children, to dream again.</p><p><strong>Her Message:&nbsp;</strong></p><p>\"When I was young, I learned embroidery because I loved it. But when I began working, I saw how little people pay for the effort. Still, I had no other choice. I had to live with it. Now, being part of this project gives me a reason to hope again. Maybe now, my children can go to bed with full stomachs.\"</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,12,21,'en',0,'<p><strong>Jamila:&nbsp; A Mother, An Artist, A Fighter</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Jamila is one of the courageous women at the heart of Marhama.</p><p>She is mother of two young children, doing everything she can to keep them safe, warm, and fed, despite facing unimaginable odds.</p><p>Born into a life of poverty in Bamiyan, Jamila grew up in a home shaped by hardship. Her father was disabled, and her mother was often ill.</p><p>From a young age, the weight of responsibility rested on her small shoulders. At just 11 years old, in 5th grade, she learned embroidery from her neighbors. It wasn\'t just a craft for her, it was survival. She balanced school, working on farm, and embroidery work to help support her parents and siblings.</p><p>Her resilience led her all the way to Kabul University, a dream many girls in her situation are never allowed to dream. Even while attending university, she continued to stitch late into the night just to afford her education. She carried hope in every thread, hope for a future she could build for herself and her family. But after the collapse of the system, her dreams were abruptly halted. She is no longer allowed to work. With no steady income and two children to feed, Jamila tried to find occasional embroidery work in her neighborhood, though it barely pays.</p><p>Joining Marhama project means more than just income for Jamila. It has brought back a sense of purpose, of dignity.</p><p>It is a light in the darkness, a chance to feed her children, to dream again.</p><p><strong>Her Message:&nbsp;</strong></p><p>\"When I was young, I learned embroidery because I loved it. But when I began working, I saw how little people pay for the effort. Still, I had no other choice. I had to live with it. Now, being part of this project gives me a reason to hope again. Maybe now, my children can go to bed with full stomachs.\"</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,13,22,'en',0,'<p><strong>Khal-Begum: A Provider, An Artist, A Dreamer</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Khal-Begum is the backbone of a family of ten. Her husband, once the provider, can no longer work due to illness. Her young son, too young for such burdens, spends his days as a street vendor, helping his mother feed their family with whatever little he earns. They live in a rented home where every corner reminds her of rising costs and unanswered prayers. To survive, Khal-Begum turns to the only thing she knows, her handcraft.</p><p>She does handcraft whenever there\'s demand, but it comes and goes, never steady, never enough.</p><p>But behind all this quiet labor is a woman who dreams.She dreams of her daughters studying without fear.</p><p>She dreams of her children growing up free from hunger. And she dreams of being recognized through her handcraft, not for fame, but because she holds a deep and personal passion for the art she creates.</p><p>Each piece is a part of her. A reflection of care, culture, and survival. Joining Marhama Project felt like her silent hopes were finally heard. Here, her work is honored, and her hands are no longer invisible.</p><p>Here, her art matters!</p><p><strong>Her Message:&nbsp;</strong></p><p>Her message to Afghan women, especially artisans like herself:</p><p>\"Embrace the art your hands can create. Show the world its beauty. Let your work speak for you.\"</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,14,23,'en',0,'<p><strong>Sharefa: A Mother\'s Hands, A Woman\'s Strength</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>In a small, rented home filled with struggles and hopes of 13 family members, Sharefa rises with the sun and works tirelessly until it sets. She is not just a mother, she is a provider, a protector, and a quiet force of resilience.Living with heart disease and diabetes, Sharefa faces daily pain and exhaustion. But there is no time to rest. Despite her health challenges and without access to proper treatment or medication, she pours her energy into sewing and handcrafting, skills she learned not for pleasure, but out of necessity.</p><p>Years ago, her children had no choice but to work as street vendors to support their schooling and put food on the table. Watching them carry the weight of survival at such a young age ignited something in her. She vowed to change their future.And so, with trembling hands and unwavering resolve, she began learning the art of sewing and handcrafts.</p><p>Today, Sharefa is the backbone of her family. Along with other women in her household, she works full-time, turning thread and fabric into hope. Still, her earnings are not enough to cover her medical needs or ease the constant financial stress. But Sharefa dreams of something simple, something powerful, a stable income. Not for luxury, but so she can afford medicine and live with dignity. Through the Marhama project, she sees the first light of that dream. She is hopeful that this opportunity will bring not only financial stability but also peace of mind.</p><p>Sharefa\'s story is one of courage, proof that even in hardship. a woman\'s will can be stronger than her circumstances. Through her hands, she\'s not only sewing fabric, she\'s stitching a future, one thread at a time.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Her Message:&nbsp;</strong></p><p>Her message to Afghan women:</p><p>\"Don\'t let your gender be an excuse to be dependent on others. Work through the abilities and talent you have. Don\'t wait for someone else to provide for you.\"</p>','','basic_html'),
('people',0,14,24,'en',0,'<p><strong>Sharefa: A Mother\'s Hands, A Woman\'s Strength</strong></p><p>In a small, rented home filled with struggles and hopes of 13 family members, Sharefa rises with the sun and works tirelessly until it sets. She is not just a mother, she is a provider, a protector, and a quiet force of resilience.Living with heart disease and diabetes, Sharefa faces daily pain and exhaustion. But there is no time to rest. Despite her health challenges and without access to proper treatment or medication, she pours her energy into sewing and handcrafting, skills she learned not for pleasure, but out of necessity.</p><p>Years ago, her children had no choice but to work as street vendors to support their schooling and put food on the table. Watching them carry the weight of survival at such a young age ignited something in her. She vowed to change their future.And so, with trembling hands and unwavering resolve, she began learning the art of sewing and handcrafts.</p><p>Today, Sharefa is the backbone of her family. Along with other women in her household, she works full-time, turning thread and fabric into hope. Still, her earnings are not enough to cover her medical needs or ease the constant financial stress. But Sharefa dreams of something simple, something powerful, a stable income. Not for luxury, but so she can afford medicine and live with dignity. Through the Marhama project, she sees the first light of that dream. She is hopeful that this opportunity will bring not only financial stability but also peace of mind.</p><p>Sharefa\'s story is one of courage, proof that even in hardship. a woman\'s will can be stronger than her circumstances. Through her hands, she\'s not only sewing fabric, she\'s stitching a future, one thread at a time.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Her Message:&nbsp;</strong></p><p>Her message to Afghan women:</p><p>\"Don\'t let your gender be an excuse to be dependent on others. Work through the abilities and talent you have. Don\'t wait for someone else to provide for you.\"</p>','','basic_html'),
